they came here to escape the horrors of  war but now the fighting is largely done  their future does not look any less  bleak this one tent is shared by four  families from afghanistan's northern  province of tajar like maryam a widowed  mother of six who says she had to sell  possessions to move here and escape the  conflict  no one helped us when we came here we  sold the tv and a carpet and have been  economizing like eating less but the  money ran out three days ago we're not  in good shape  located on the outskirts of kabul this  open piece of land was overrun with  displaced people as the taliban closed  in on the capital and as the government  attempted to move them to more organized  camps  with the collapse of that government has  come the end of any official help adding  to kabul's growing crisis of people  displaced by war  their journeys began from places where  the conflict has raged around  afghanistan in recent months and the  taliban victory has brought a degree of  stability but for many still no clearer  idea of where to go next  on the other side of this shared tent  khulam hazarat wants to take his family  of nine back to their home  but he doesn't know if the house he is  still paying rent on is even standing  we don't have any money left to go back  and set up again in our old place if the  government can look after us that's  better  right now that government doesn't exist  yet when it does it'll be up to the  taliban to provide support  and tackle the many other crises facing  afghanistan rob mcbride al jazeera kabul
 

